---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import InteriorHero from '../../components/InteriorHero.astro';
import SectionShell from '../../components/SectionShell.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="Concrete Preparation and Repair Edmonton | Final Finish" description="Plan concrete preparation, repair and levelling in Edmonton by assessing slab condition, moisture questions and the finish scope before coating." canonicalPath="/services/concrete-solutions">
  <SiteHeader />
  <main id="main-content">
    <InteriorHero eyebrow="Inspect · Prepare · Finish" title="A finished floor can only be as considered as the concrete beneath it." lead="Concrete repair, surface levelling and mechanical preparation are decisions about the slab in front of us, the proposed system and the room’s future use." />
    <SectionShell>
      <p class="section-kicker">Begin with evidence</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">Condition comes before a coating recommendation</h2>
      <p class="section-lead">Visible cracks, spalls, hollow-sounding areas, patches, joints, old coatings, oil, adhesive, low spots and water history should be recorded before a decorative finish is selected. Some conditions are cosmetic; others need a different repair or a qualified evaluation. A coating is not a substitute for identifying recurring water entry, movement or unsound concrete.</p>
      <p>The <a href="/resources/concrete-cracks-spalling-floor-coating">concrete condition guide</a> and <a href="/resources/concrete-repair-mortars-floor-coatings">repair-mortar guide</a> explain the questions that separate a visible defect from a defined repair scope. Ask how discoveries after preparation will be photographed, priced and approved.</p>
    </SectionShell>
    <SectionShell tone="stone">
      <p class="section-kicker">Preparation follows the condition</p>
      <div class="card-grid">
        <article class="editorial-card"><h3>Mechanical preparation</h3><p>Grinding may be part of preparation where the planned system calls for a clean, profiled substrate. The <a href="/resources/diamond-grinding-concrete-preparation">diamond-grinding guide</a> describes the records and edges a scope should cover.</p></article>
        <article class="editorial-card"><h3>Repair and levelling</h3><p>Cracks, damaged areas and elevation changes need their own sequence, material and acceptance questions. Do not assume a repair product also addresses active movement, drainage or every moisture condition.</p></article>
        <article class="editorial-card"><h3>Traction planning</h3><p>Traction is considered against contamination, footwear, slope, cleaning and maintenance. The intended use should be written down before a texture is selected.</p></article>
      </div>
    </SectionShell>
    <SectionShell>
      <p class="section-kicker">Keep moisture specific</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">Moisture is a system question, not a promise</h2>
      <p>Signs of dampness, efflorescence, leaks, flooding or recent washing are reasons to ask what investigation is appropriate; they do not identify a source or prove a coating outcome. If testing is proposed, request the method, locations, timing, results and the current product documentation that connects those results to the complete system. A broad “moisture barrier” label is not a substitute for that record.</p>
      <p>Read the <a href="/resources/concrete-moisture-testing-floor-coatings">concrete moisture-testing guide</a> before treating a test as a pass/fail promise. For a comparison of finishing approaches, the <a href="/resources/concrete-polishing-vs-floor-coating">polishing versus coating guide</a> keeps the decision tied to substrate, maintenance and future use.</p>
    </SectionShell>
    <SectionShell tone="stone">
      <p class="section-kicker">Write down the decisions</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">A clear scope makes room for legitimate discoveries</h2>
      <p>A useful quote identifies the observed condition, preparation, repair assumptions, system layers, texture or finish direction, excluded areas, access needs and the change process for concealed damage. It should also state what must be confirmed before each return-to-use step. The <a href="/resources/how-to-compare-floor-coating-proposals">proposal comparison guide</a> can help you compare complete scopes rather than a single material name.</p>
      <a class="button button-primary" href="/contact#quote">Discuss concrete preparation</a>
    </SectionShell>
  </main>
  <SiteFooter />
</BaseLayout>
